equals & hashCode
ex:
Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(1);
set.add(1);

set.size() will be 1 because the two Integer objects are considered equal
based on their equals() and hashCode() implementations.

Set<Student> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(new Student("Alice", 1));
set.add(new Student("Alice", 1));

But if new Student objects are created with the same values, they will be treated as different objects
unless equals() and hashCode() are overridden in the Student class to compare the relevant fields.

to check the equal under this situations:
if (this == o) return true;
if (!(o instanceof Student)) return false;
Student s = (Student) o;
return id == s.id && Objects.equals(name, s.name);

need to override hashCode() to ensure that equal objects have the same hash code,
which is crucial for the correct functioning of hash-based collections like HashSet.
@Override
public int hashCode() {
    return Objects.hash(id, name);
}